{"kind":"request","text":"claude: hugh measured our CI and ranked this third of three opportunities. It is the one with the most headroom and the least certainty, which is why the conditions ask for measurement rather than a claimed factor.\n\nWHAT HE FOUND, AND WHAT I VERIFIED. internal/docset takes 45 to 73 seconds in CI. An isolated local run under load took 150 seconds, of which TestGateNoPageIsUnableToFlare alone was 112. The cause is named in your own code: the comment at internal/docset/model_test.go:45 says signing every event is the expensive part and the flare gate needs one workroom per act it retires, so the graph is built once and copied. I read that comment at main and it is exactly right about what it is doing. The tests construct every stand-in and page through Workspace.Act, copy the Git repository for each governing act, reopen, append a retirement and snapshot.\n\nWHY THAT IS WORTH CHANGING. The behaviour actually under test is projection semantics, and workroom.Fold over records is already the repository's normal way to test that directly. Paying for signing and repository copies to observe a fold is buying something the assertions never look at.\n\nTHE TRAP I WANT AVOIDED, and it is the whole reason this is a request rather than a note. A rewrite that makes the package fast and quietly stops catching the flare defect is worse than the slow suite, and it would look like a win in every number we report. So the conditions ask you to break the flare rule and watch TestGateNoPageIsUnableToFlare fail before and after. If it does not fail after, the rewrite has removed the gate rather than accelerated it.\n\nI also want one Git-backed case kept. The fold may be the behaviour, but the adapter between records and workspace is real code that can break, and a package that only ever folds in memory would not notice.\n\nON THE NUMBERS. Hugh estimates likely more than 5x for this package and explicitly says it needs a before-and-after benchmark before claiming an exact factor. I agree, and I would add that today's measurements are not trustworthy under load: I saw a timing-sensitive test in cmd/gs fail purely because I ran two suites at once, and hugh's own local figure differs from CI by more than 2x. So measure both ends yourself, on the same machine, and say what else was running.\n\nThis is third in his ranking and last in your queue. Nothing waits on it.","body":{"conditions":"Not urgent; sequenced last, behind ba9326ae, the statusview lane and the decision-records repairs. Make internal/docset test projection semantics through workroom.Fold on in-memory records rather than through a Git-backed workspace, keeping one small Git-backed integration case to prove the adapter wiring is real. The point is that the behaviour under test is projection semantics, and the current shape pays for signing, a repository copy per governing act, a reopen, an append and a snapshot to observe it. Measure before and after and report both figures from runs you actually performed: state the package wall time at the current head and at the delivered head, each from a -count=1 run, and name the machine conditions, since load has produced figures varying by more than 3x today. Do not quote a speedup factor you did not measure. Coverage must not narrow: every behaviour the current tests pin must still be pinned, and TestGateNoPageIsUnableToFlare in particular must still fail when the flare rule is broken. Prove that by breaking the flare rule at its decision site, watching that named assertion fail, and reverting; a faster suite that no longer catches the defect is a regression sold as an improvement. Keep at least one case that exercises the real Git-backed path end to end, so a fault in the adapter between records and workspace cannot pass unnoticed. If some case genuinely cannot be expressed against workroom.Fold, leave it Git-backed and say which and why in the artifact rather than dropping it. Exact clean pushed head, per-path artifacts, gofmt, build, vet and go test ./... -count=1 green, independent review. Do not merge.","severity":"normal","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}